(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-10"] = {"16.1":{"s":"Food supplies nutrients that give energy and help the body grow and repair itself. The nutrients are carbohydrates, lipids (fats), proteins, vitamins, minerals and water. Food quality is judged by flavour, smell, texture, colour and spoilage by microbes. Oxygen, enzymes and microbes spoil stored food, so it is kept airtight or cold, and some packed foods contain antioxidants as additives to last longer.","w":["Keep apart the roles: carbohydrates and fats give energy, proteins build and repair the body.","A food additive has to be safe within the permitted amount; artificial sweeteners are not a source of energy in the same way as sugar."]},"16.2":{"s":"Some chemical compounds act as medicines. Analgesics reduce pain, and antipyretics bring down fever; paracetamol and aspirin are common examples. Antimicrobials kill or stop the growth of microbes, and include antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants. Antiseptics are mild enough to apply to living tissue, but disinfectants are used on objects and are too strong for skin. A drug must be taken only in the right dose.","w":["Antiseptic is used on the body (such as dilute iodine); disinfectant on floors and instruments (such as phenol in strong solution).","Antibiotics act on bacteria, not on viruses."]},"16.3":{"s":"Cleansing agents remove dirt, which is mostly oily and sticks to the surface. Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids, and detergents are synthetic compounds with a similar structure. A molecule has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail. In water the tails surround the oily dirt and form tiny balls called micelles, which are washed away. Soaps do not work well in hard water, but detergents do.","w":["Soap forms a scum with the calcium and magnesium ions of hard water; most detergents do not.","Detergents with branched chains are not broken down by bacteria and cause water pollution; straight-chain ones are biodegradable."]}};
